import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<String> tasks = new ArrayDeque<>();
        tasks.offerLast("email");
        tasks.offerFirst("server down");
        tasks.offerLast("lunch order");
        tasks.offerFirst("fire alarm");
        System.out.println("Tasks: " + tasks);
        System.out.println("First: " + tasks.peekFirst());
        System.out.println("Last: " + tasks.peekLast());

        String[] phrases = {"Never odd or even", "Open the door"};
        for (String phrase : phrases) {
            System.out.println(phrase + ": " + isPalindrome(phrase));
        }
    }

    static boolean isPalindrome(String phrase) {
        Deque<Character> chars = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : phrase.toCharArray()) {
            if (!Character.isWhitespace(c)) {
                chars.offerLast(Character.toLowerCase(c));
            }
        }
        while (chars.size() > 1) {
            char first = chars.pollFirst();
            char last = chars.pollLast();
            if (first != last) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}

steps.body:
A `PriorityQueue` is a heap: `poll()` always removes the smallest element in natural order, whatever order you added them in. Use it when you keep needing the next best item.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();   // min-heap
pq.offer(5);
pq.offer(1);
System.out.println(pq.peek());  // 1
System.out.println(pq.poll());  // 1
System.out.println(pq.size());  // 1
```

For a max-heap, pass `Collections.reverseOrder()` to the constructor. `Comparator.reverseOrder()` from the Lambdas lesson does the same. For arrays such as `int[]{id, priority}`, pass a comparator lambda with the same two-key shape you gave `Arrays.sort` in the Lambdas lesson. A heap does not keep arrival order among equal priorities, so add a tie-breaker that fixes their order:

```java
PriorityQueue<int[]> tasks = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> {
    if (a[1] != b[1]) {
        return Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]);
    }
    return Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]);
});
tasks.offer(new int[]{2, 5});
tasks.offer(new int[]{1, 5});
System.out.println(tasks.poll()[0]); // 1, the tie-breaker picks the lower id
```

Use `Integer.compare`, never `a[1] - b[1]`, which can overflow.

Gotcha: printing a `PriorityQueue` or looping over it with an enhanced for loop shows heap order, not sorted order. Only `poll()` in a loop gives sorted output. Also never print a `PriorityQueue<int[]>` directly: format each polled array with `p[0]` and `p[1]`.

